    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "that makes sense. i'm mostly worried about those weird edge cases though—like when the UI looks fine to us but someone using a screen reader or high contrast mode hits a total wall. are there specific ways to test for those \"hidden\" accessibility bugs that aren't immediately obvious just by looking at the design?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "To find those hidden bugs, you must test using actual assistive technologies, not just visual inspection. Utilize screen readers like NVDA or VoiceOver to navigate the entire application flow to catch missing labels or incorrect focus order. Additionally, simulate high contrast modes or use browser extensions to actively check how colors and focus indicators change under adverse viewing conditions. These hands-on testing methods reveal issues that static checks simply miss.",
      "source": "target",
