    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "yeah i'll look into nvda. it just feels like there's always these little things that pop up right at the end of a sprint that break everything. like, what's a good way to categorize these kinds of annoying, unpredictable interface glitches so the dev team knows they're dealing with something that seriously ruins the flow rather than just a tiny tweak?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Categorize these critical, flow-breaking issues under the umbrella of \"Blockers\" or \"Severity 1: Usability Failure.\" Clearly document the steps to reproduce the glitch, noting *which* accessibility method (e.g., screen reader navigation, keyboard input) exposed the flaw. Instead of labeling it a \"tweak,\" describe the negative impact—for instance, \"User cannot complete checkout form\"—to clearly show it ruins the user flow.",
      "source": "target",
